{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791577866,"nonce":"2OR0YukvPyPqhCKmoYLsJ8vwNT4=","message":"Batched reads landed in 8f00783, with blinc_abi 2d61d75 and Blinc d12f5917. CI pins 2d61d75 and is green.\n\n**What changed**\n- GraphContext keys host effects by raw EffectId (no tag map); end_effect_with_reads takes raw signal ids; removing a host effect applies at once.\n- The addon maps the graph's index for a due effect (low half of its raw id) to our slot index with a Vec, and takeDue reports slot indexes; JS keeps effects by slot index.\n- While an effect runs, signal.get() writes the signal's key into a Float64Array the context allocated and registered once (setScratch: address from napi_get_typedarray_info, kept alive by a napi reference, registered again when it grows). No native call per read; a repeat of the previous key is skipped. endEffect(key, count) resolves the keys through the slot table and passes the raw ids to end_effect_with_reads. Computed reads still call track natively.\n- peek(), and get() outside any computed or effect, make no native call: the JS Signal and its context are disposed whenever the native signal is, so their checks stand in.\n- JS keeps no graph, subscriptions, dirtiness or scheduling: only the current run's read keys. Due order and re-queueing come from Blinc.\n\n**Measured** (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, release addons; \"before\" is 7cdc8f8 (Blinc 0e4ffada), its dist and addon copied aside and run alternately with \"after\"; two rounds each)\n\nbenchmarks/reactive-flush.mjs (median of 31; ns per effect, or per write and effect for batches):\n- one write, 1,000 effects: before 1049, 1030; after 830, 788\n- one write, 10,000 effects: before 1049, 1011; after 787, 787\n- batch of 1,000 writes, one effect each: before 1313, 1320; after 1129, 1162\n- batch of 10,000 writes: before 1353, 1328; after 1054, 1023\n- 1,000 effects each reading 1 signal, rerun by one batch: before 967, 1012; after 736, 741\n- reading 5: before 2190, 2102; after 957, 956\n- reading 20: before 6701, 6624; after 1602, 1608\n\nbenchmarks/reactive-memory.mjs, effect, 100,000 items (footprint per item alive [V8 heap]; then after six make/dispose rounds):\n- before: 1276 [389]; 1351, 2158, 2181, 2182, 2183, 2201 and 1188 [389]; 1164, 1691, 1954, 1966, 1967, 1968\n- after: 856 [285]; 823, 1324, 1599, 1848, 1850, 1851 and 954 [285]; 923, 1371, 1642, 1895, 1896, 1900\n\nAlive per effect is now below the closure-effect figure from before host effects (about 1,150). After disposal the rounds still settle near 1,850-1,900 B per item, above the closure control's 420; most of that is capacity kept by the graph and the slot tables, not live items.","files":null}]}